A||||20||Kage86GT4SP||Pep Boys "$900 to fix your AC."
Z||000000||Kage86GT4SP||08-15-99||12:03 AM||||So... Waddaya think?<p>They say the compressor is siezed.  (It turned for me... but not freely.) They say there is a hole in the condensor, but then they say there is 40lbs of pressure in both the high and the low side.<p>Comments?<p>K.<p>(Who said, in reply: "Not only `NO!,' but `HELL NO!'")
Z||000001||Cooter||08-15-99||09:01 AM||||If the compressor has locked up, a replacement should cost about $150-200 US. If the system has 40 psi on low/high side, how can it have a hole in it? Wouldnt the freon leak out and leave 0 psi? I know others will have comments here, but this is what I would do- Find someone who will use a "leak sniffer" to verify the leaking condensor. Call or find Carolina Auto Parts on the web for a compressor, receiver/drier, orfice tube, 134a conversion kit and a condensor if needed. Any questionable hoses should be replaced and all o-rings should be replaced since you already have the system broken down. I think all these parts would cost less than $900. 
Z||000002||batboy||08-15-99||10:07 AM||||The A/C business nowadays is a big racket. R12 Freon is being smuggled into the country illegally. Remaining supplies in warehouses are being stolen at alarming rates. This recycling certification law seems to promote questionable politics and favoritism. The mystery and contradicting stories about how to convert an old A/C system over to the new style refridgerant. I'm telling you, it's a big organized rip-off going on right now.
Z||000003||terryk||08-15-99||04:43 PM||||It's not that out of line. let me suggest you take it to a GM dealer and have them repair it. It will be a lifetime warranty which is worth it. Likely to be the same cost. My Trans Sport locked and disinegrated the compressor. $850, but I never pay again.<p>For fun, when you pay the bill, ask for all of the old parts back. Look at the bill, and if they charged you for freon, ask for your old freon back. I did that and after explaining it was the law to return all old parts on request, I got the freon for free. They were the one's that said it wasn't leaking!<p>You will get some interesting arguments.
Z||000004||Cooter||08-15-99||05:31 PM||||Terryk- that is a good one, I will have to remember that one! With that kind of attitude, I bet that you can give a telemarketer hell too. LOL
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||08-15-99||06:51 PM||||:::scratching head::: So, is it possible to buy one of those 20 dollar R134A conversions and then it's possible to run R134A in the old R12 system? I just don't think it's possible. 
Z||000006||Cooter||08-15-99||09:57 PM||||SCCA- what do you mean???? I have 134 in my fiero and didnt change anything at all- just added the fittings for 134 and it cools great.
Z||000007||DJRice||08-16-99||07:48 AM||||When I bought my 87 V6, the only work it needed was AC Repair.  I have a well trusted mechanic who was able to refit my AC system for R134.  As I understand it, some o-rings need to be replaced and thats about it.<p>As for the compressors going bad, usually what happens is that the death of the compressor will result in some metal shavings throughout your AC system. When that happens you usually have to replace the condensor and/or drier to prevent the metal shavings from ruining the new compressor.  <p>It all comes down to finding a mechanic that you trust.  <p>All that having been said, If you take your Fiero to a mechanic and he says "I'll take a look at it, but I usually have trouble getting parts for those foreign cars."  Go to  a different mechanic.
Z||000008||DJRice||08-16-99||07:51 AM||||Ok I dont guess that statement holds true for  Cliff, and technically I guess its the same for the guys in Canada, but you get my point.
Z||000009||theogre||08-16-99||11:18 AM||||the "standard" is that if the compressor fails, flush the system with the orifice tube and ACC/dryer removed. Then replace the orifice tube and Accumulator/dryer unit . You should be able to flush the rest of the system, and not replace it.<p>The reason for this is mostly as mentioned above. the compressor failure often polutes the system. If the compressor scrap gets into the orifice tube it can screw up the high side pressure, and even damage the system. Also at this point, the ACC/Dryer is old, and may be poluted. The new Acc/dryer will have 134 friendly desicant on it. This also removes the last of the old oil from the system.<p>the 134 Kit you are refering to still requires the old R12 to be removed first. Don't mix the R12 with the 134.
Z||000010||theogre||08-16-99||02:04 PM||||P.S. and yes,,, if you are doing a 134 conversion at the same time, pull the rest apart and change out the old O rings. you should really do this even if you are replacing the R12 just to prevent leaks.
Z||000011||BOILERMAKER||08-16-99||08:05 PM||||I changed out my R12 to R134.  The only O-rings I changed were the ones that were showing.  I had an open system and the 2 O-rings were where the system was open.  I have an email of a guy in S. Carolina and he is a rep for ... I don't have the name handy, but will tomorrow.<p>I think it was around $100 and he supplied the flush (1 gallon) ester oil (more than 9 oz) and the fittings and the gun to flush the system with.  It was quite easy.  I have been hesitating to make any comments about this changeover, but it's been around 2 months in the summer (alot of the weather has been 80 - 90+) and it hasn't skipped a beat.<p>I am quite satisfied with this guy's product.  I will have his email handy along with the name of the company tomorrow for anyone who wants it.  By the way, he is an instructor for refrigerant changeovers.
Z||000012||RFMike||08-17-99||10:30 PM||||I took my Fiero to a Pontiac dealer located across the street from where I work.  AC had completely quit cooling.  Told them to check for leaks, and if there weren't any, to recharge it.  The guy at the counter said it would be about $100.  When I picked it up, the bill was $154, with $20 for freon recovery.  How do you recover from an empty system?  I bitched a little, but paid.<br>When I got in the brake pedal was really soft.  I pumped it, and it came up a little, but not much.  I found the same guy and he said, "Oh yeah, the mechanic said the pedal was spongy."  Thanks, moron.<br>I went back to the car, pumped it, and looked for leaks.  None. Zippo.<br>Found the moron, told him I had personally checked the brake fluid three days earlier (true), and there was no way it had leaked out in the 5 hours they had it.  Thats when the arguing really started.  I finally said,"Get me some DOT3 NOW, and I won't call the TV newsies about your little scam.  Don't get it, and they'll all be here in the morning."  He went and got it.<br>I still get pissed when I think about it.  At the rates that are charged, plenty of money gets made for honest work.  I do most of my mechanical work, but some things I either don't have the expertise for, or can't fit in the time for.  Sucks, when you pay somebody for something, and they still try to rip you.
Z||000013||DJRice||08-18-99||07:10 AM||||Dude, call the TV News anyway.  Most of them now have the consumer protection reporter that does the investigations into scams.  Let 'em have it<br>
Z||000014||stsmithgt||08-20-99||10:01 PM||||Having it cool is great.  I have an 86 GT which I needed the A/C worked on.  They said my compressor was leaking so they replaced it for about 900.00.  Did they see me coming or what?  BUT that didn't entirly fix the problem.  after about 5 visits, I finally got them to replace all the O-rings that were leaking throughout the system.  For being profefionals, they sure had tubular vision.  It finally cost close to 1400.00 *#!!?#$$$%fu!!  dollars to get out of there.  Wayside garage in Seaside CA.  and they are the better ones in the area. <IMG SRC="/ubb/frown.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>  Being in a tuorist area really screws up the cost of everything.  It pays to have patiants and to search in different locations, which I did'nt.  It works great though.
Z||000015||Fiero64||08-28-99||06:40 PM||||A/C problems can be a pain in the butt.  When it comes to retrofitting here's my opinion:  R-134a has smaller particles thand R-12 so consequently if you do a retrofit and DO NOT change all of your o-rings you may have a system that will start leaking due to the fact that the smaller particles can now get past the old worn o-rings. The problem with using the old R-12 compressor is that the smaller R-143a particles tend to loosen up any crap in your A/C system and contaminate everything.  After a retrofit you can expect to possibly change your compressor/ACC-dryer/oriface in about 6 months.  R-12 runs about $40 per pound as opposed to the $15 per pound for the R-134a, so in the long run R-134a is going to be cheaper to use but, in my opinion, less effective........ 
Z||000016||theogre||08-28-99||07:46 PM||||If you are doing all the orings and such, make sure you flush the mess out with the correct flush agent. Some are better than others for conversion. Any standard air gun with a liquid injector and rubber nose can be used for pushing the flush thru. The quart of flush will run you less than 10$.
Z||000017||theogre||08-28-99||07:51 PM||||BTW.... I priced the compressor, ACC/Dryer, orifice, and castrol conversion kit(the one with new oil, rings/seals, and fittings) at Advance Auto (for my 87, the parts for the pre 87's are a bit more) The total was about 320$us. The R134 brings it up around 360 or something like that.<p>It will take all day at least to change everything, flush, assemble, evac, and charge.
Z||000018||Cooter||08-28-99||08:21 PM||||Question??? If 134 has smaller molecules than r12, then why does 134 make a higher head pressure than the same amount of r12 on a conversion? If the stuff is smaller, wouldn't it go through the orfice tube easier and have less head pressure? I have wondered about this after converting several r12 cars to 134 and only used 1.5 pounds of 134 where the car needed 2 pounds of r12.
Z||000019||Greg Piet||08-29-99||02:11 AM||||I got mine w/o any r 12 in it.<br>I evacuated it and got some r-12 and filled the puppy up.<br>It worked fine for a month then after a tranny shop got done with my car it was out of refrigerant.<br>I didnt want to overuse my source of r-12 so  did re veacuated it, and used a $50 r134 conversion kit and a can of the magical stop leak and detector for r134.<br>ever since then (another three months) it has been working even better than my r 12 (in las vegas heat).<br>The high/low pressures are all within spec and the compressor has been well behaved.<br>With my success i would highly reccomend that  procedure if you are afraid or too busy to change all the o rings and dont have the $$$ to get it done.  Out by me the AC shops want $100 minimum just to fill the sucker not including looking for leaks or fixing anything.  <br>Also as my refrigerant expert friend tells me "ALWAYS run the ac at least once a week even if you dont need to!"
Z||000020||AL||09-03-99||08:45 PM||||well let me put my two cents in.when my cars need freon i just get my little tool out, and take a nice drive to tj mexico. i go to the first auto parts i see, and buy real freon in a bottle. then i connect it to my system and presto it is recharged with the good stuff. then i just drive back home with the coldest air possible.and all ipay for 5 cans are about 3 bucks.
